{
  "ranking": [
    {
      "id": "patch_000305187b49",
      "failure_port": "input_axis_tready",
      "source_line": 105,
      "mechanism": "The public failure shows input_axis_tready=1 at cycle 4 (expected 0) before any data appears at output_axis_tdata. In the original code, input_axis_tready = ~full with no reset qualifier, so it asserts ready while the three-cycle input_rst_sync chain is still flushing. patch_000305187b49 changes the ready gate from '(~full)' to '(~full) && (!async_rst)', preventing ready assertion during the asynchronous reset window that spans cycles 0-3. This directly targets the earliest failing port and the earliest cycle mismatch."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_3d75e9177240",
      "failure_port": "output_axis_tdata",
      "source_line": 110,
      "mechanism": "The first output_axis_tdata mismatch occurs at cycle 8 (actual 1, expected 2). The output clock domain holds rd_ptr and rd_ptr_gray in output_rst_sync3 until cycle 3, then begins advancing on 'read' cycles. The cross-clock synchronization uses async_rst as the sole guard for the output_rst_sync registers at line 110. When input_rst_sync3 is still asserted, the input domain is frozen but the output domain may start advancing because async_rst has de-asserted. patch_3d75e9177240 qualifies the output_rst_sync reset condition with '(!input_rst_sync3)', forcing the output domain to remain in reset until the input domain has fully released its reset chain. This couples the two reset domains and addresses the earliest output data mismatch."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_e4d895e08cad",
      "failure_port": "input_axis_tready",
      "source_line": 105,
      "mechanism": "The ready gate '(~full)' is evaluated combinationally from wr_ptr_gray and rd_ptr_gray_sync2. During reset flushing, wr_ptr_gray is held at 0 by input_rst_sync3, but rd_ptr_gray_sync2 may still be transitioning. patch_e4d895e08cad qualifies the ready condition with '(!input_rst_sync3)', holding input_axis_tready low until the input domain's own three-cycle reset chain has completed. This is a minimal single-site edit on the direct failure port with the same cycle-4 mismatch as patch_000305187b49 but using the local reset state instead of the async input."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_c9a8474a8897",
      "failure_port": "input_axis_tready",
      "source_line": 148,
      "mechanism": "At line 148 the wr_ptr update is gated by 'if (input_rst_sync3)'. While input_rst_sync3=1 the write pointer is frozen, yet the ready gate at line 105 does not observe input_rst_sync3. If a spurious write were to occur during the reset window, full could de-assert and incorrectly raise ready. patch_c9a8474a8897 changes the write-side condition from 'input_rst_sync3' to '(input_rst_sync3) && (!read)', which does not directly affect the ready port and is ranked lower because it targets a condition whose direct_failure_target is false and whose failure anchor is the output data port, not the failing ready port."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_adc7330dd4b7",
      "failure_port": "input_axis_tready",
      "source_line": 136,
      "mechanism": "The input_rst_sync register chain (line 136) is reset by 'async_rst' alone. patch_adc7330dd4b7 qualifies this with '(input_rst_sync3) && (output_rst_sync3)', making the input reset chain depend on the output reset chain as well. This is a cross-domain coupling at the reset generator rather than at the ready gate. It is ranked after the direct ready-gate edits because it modifies a register update condition whose direct_failure_target is false and whose cited failure port is input_axis_tready, but the static dependency shows six cell edges to that port."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_369cf8cc07c8",
      "failure_port": "input_axis_tready",
      "source_line": 139,
      "mechanism": "The write pointer update (line 139) is conditioned on 'write' and 'input_rst_sync3'. patch_369cf8cc07c8 changes the guard from 'write' to '(write) && (!input_rst_sync3)', which is functionally equivalent to the existing 'else if (write)' under the 'if (input_rst_sync3)' but makes the condition explicit. The failure anchor is input_axis_tready at cycle 4; the edit does not change the ready gate directly and its direct_failure_target is false."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_92befdfe800b",
      "failure_port": "input_axis_tready",
      "source_line": 136,
      "mechanism": "Similar to patch_adc7330dd4b7, this qualifies the input_rst_sync reset condition with '(input_rst_sync3) && (write)' instead of 'async_rst'. The replacement ties the input reset release to the write handshake, which is unrelated to the ready assertion failure. Ranked last among the cited candidates because it introduces a data-dependent term into the reset synchronizer without addressing the ready gate."
    }
  ],
  "explanation": "The earliest mismatches are input_axis_tready at cycle 4 and output_axis_tdata at cycle 8. The top-ranked patches directly qualify the ready gate (input_axis_tready = ~full) with reset-state terms that hold ready low during the three-cycle input_rst_sync flushing window. patch_000305187b49 uses the asynchronous reset input; patch_e4d895e08cad uses the local input_rst_sync3; both target the direct failure port at the failing cycle. The first output data mismatch is addressed by patch_3d75e9177240, which couples the output domain's reset release to the input domain's reset completion, preventing premature pointer advancement in the output clock domain. Remaining candidates modify reset-synchronizer conditions or write-pointer guards that do not directly drive the failing ports and have direct_failure_target=false."
}
